---
scope: src/service/install
status: misaligned
description: >
  Install service — the consumer-agnostic core that turns a cold project into an
  AIDE-ready project, shared by the `aide_init` MCP tool and the npx CLI. Writes
  non-existing files silently, only pausing on overwrites or inherently
  interactive decisions (brain root path, MCP config merge, IDE choice). The
  brain MCP entry's command/args are derived from the user-owned brain.aide via
  parseBrainAide — the package never owns launcher bytes.
intent: >
  The install service is the shared core that turns a cold project
  into an AIDE-ready project. It is consumer-agnostic: the same
  service is invoked by the `aide_init` MCP tool (when the user is
  talking to an agent connected to this server) and by the npx CLI
  (when the user has nothing connected yet). The two consumers
  differ only in their wire shape; the planning, idempotency,
  prescriptions, and category structure live here. The service is
  designed to be fast and unobtrusive: when a category has nothing
  on disk to overwrite, it just writes the files and reports what it
  did. The consumer pauses the user only when the install would
  destroy or clobber something they already have, or when a decision
  cannot be resolved from the filesystem alone.

  The rule is: confirmation is reserved for overwrites and for
  inherent decisions. A category whose plan contains only would-create
  steps runs silently and reports its results. A category whose plan
  contains would-overwrite steps pauses and asks the user — category-
  by-category, using the consumer's native pause shape — before
  applying. Inherent decisions — framework detection confirmation,
  brain root location, MCP server-merge into an existing config, and
  IDE choice — always prompt the user regardless of disk state,
  because filesystem contents cannot resolve them.

  The categories are: framework detection, methodology pointer stub,
  methodology doc set, pipeline slash commands, pipeline agent
  definitions, pipeline skill templates, MCP server registration
  (aide), brain provisioning, MCP server registration
  (brain), and IDE configuration. The brain MCP entry is keyed
  `brain` per the project-root settlement, and its command/args are
  derived from the user-owned brain.aide via parseBrainAide — the
  package does not own launcher bytes, does not infer backend
  identity from MCP entries, and does not enumerate or dispatch on a
  connector enum. The framework itself is storage-agnostic; the
  install ships with a default brain.aide template (sourced from the
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper), which the user gets unless they
  edit it. Each category still reports what it would do (create,
  overwrite, skip, already exists), and the consumer only surfaces the
  category to the user when an overwrite is pending or an inherent
  decision is needed.

  The brain layer is not optional — AIDE is opinionated about this.
  The pipeline needs a brain to persist research and retros. But the
  user chooses where it lives: they can connect to an existing brain
  root anywhere on their system, or have the install create a new one
  at a location they specify. The consumer interviews the user to
  resolve the brain root path; the install service never silently
  assumes a sibling folder or falls back to a default. This interview
  happens regardless of disk state because the brain root path is an
  inherent decision, not a file-write decision.

  MCP config changes are returned as prescriptions — the key name and
  server entry the config needs — not written directly by the install
  service. The consumer reads the existing MCP config, merges the
  prescribed entry into it, and writes the result. The user sees the
  merge before it happens. This ensures the consumer has full
  visibility into what exists in the config and the user's other
  servers are never at risk, even when the config is malformed.

  After first scaffold, `.aide/config/` is user-owned directory-wide.
  The install service may create the directory and seed initial
  contents on a cold start, but on every subsequent run no path
  under `.aide/config/` is ever returned as `would-overwrite`,
  patched, or migrated by the install pipeline — adding a new brain,
  changing backend command/args, or editing brain.aide is a
  user-side edit, never a code-level extension.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The install service returns a structured plan containing a
      categorized list of steps, each with a status (would-create,
      would-overwrite, would-skip, exists) and the data the consumer
      needs to execute that step (file paths, content, MCP entries).
      The consumer interprets this plan and decides per-category
      whether user interaction is needed. Same plan shape regardless
      of which entry point invoked the service.
    - Framework auto-detection picks the right framework from files
      on disk, and the consumer confirms the detection with the user
      before proceeding. Framework choice is an inherent decision —
      the prompt happens regardless of disk state.
    - "A category whose plan contains only would-create steps (no
      existing files on disk) is applied silently by the consumer:
      no confirmation, just write and report. The install is
      intentionally fast for the common cold-start path where there
      is nothing to preserve."
    - "A category whose plan contains at least one would-overwrite
      step pauses the consumer category-by-category using its
      native structured-prompt mechanism (AskUserQuestion in the
      MCP tool path, stdin prompts in the CLI path). The consumer
      names the files that would be overwritten so the user can
      make an informed choice, then either applies the whole
      category or skips it per the user's selection."
    - "Inherent decisions — brain root location, MCP server merge
      into an existing config, and IDE choice — always prompt the
      user regardless of what is on disk. These are not file-write
      decisions; filesystem state cannot resolve them. The fast-path
      rule applies only to pure file-create categories."
    - For MCP config, the install service returns a prescription —
      the server key name and the entry object — instead of writing
      the file. The consumer reads the existing config, shows the
      user the planned merge, and writes. If the config is malformed
      JSON, the consumer tells the user and asks how to proceed
      rather than silently clobbering.
    - "The brain root interview is consumer-driven and always
      happens. The consumer asks the user where their brain root is,
      offering three paths: discover an existing brain root (from env
      var, known locations, or user input), create a new brain root
      at a specified location, or connect to an existing brain root
      at a user-provided path. The brain is always provisioned —
      there is no skip-brain option. Once the path is resolved, the
      install service reports whether the brain root needs scaffolding
      and the consumer applies it."
    - "Wiring the brain end-to-end from a resolved brainPath requires
      consumers to invoke the install service twice with distinct
      category values: once with \"category: mcp\" to apply the brain
      MCP prescription to .mcp.json (under the fixed `brain` key,
      with command/args derived from the scaffolded brain.aide bytes
      via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs in provisionBrain — not
      from a hardcoded launcher constant or a code-level backend
      registry), and once with \"category: brain\" to scaffold brain
      root directories and seed content files. The split is
      intentional and load-bearing: the mcp category is
      prescription-only (the consumer merges the returned entry into
      .mcp.json because the install service never writes that file
      directly), while the brain category is direct filesystem work
      (the install service creates brain root directories and seeds
      content itself). Neither category alone fully wires the brain —
      both must run for the brain to be reachable by pipeline agents."
    - "After the initial scaffold, `.aide/config/` is user-owned
      directory-wide. The install pipeline may create the directory
      and seed initial contents (e.g., the default brain.aide
      template) on a cold start when the path does not exist, but on
      every subsequent run no path under `.aide/config/` is ever
      returned as `would-overwrite`, patched, or migrated by the
      install service. Adding a new brain, changing the backend's
      command/args, or editing brain.aide is a user-side edit; the
      package never re-touches user-owned config bytes."
    - IDE configuration is presented as optional — the consumer asks
      whether the user wants Zed and/or VS Code configuration, and
      only applies what they confirm. IDE choice is an inherent
      decision and always prompts.
    - Every step is idempotent. Re-running the install action on a
      fully initialized project — through either entry point —
      returns a plan where every step shows "exists" and the
      consumer reports that nothing needs to change, without asking
      a single question.
    - The install service's response is structured data the consumer
      interprets, not a pre-formatted summary the consumer parrots.
      The consumer composes its own output (conversational for the
      MCP tool, terminal-friendly for the CLI) from the structured
      data, tailored to the user's context.
    - "The default response (no category parameter) is small enough
      for the consumer to comprehend in a single read — metadata
      only, no file content — and includes overwrite status per
      category so the consumer can decide which categories need
      prompts. The MCP-tool consumer is the primary driver of the
      no-content-in-default-response rule because the agent reading
      the response has a token budget; the CLI consumer benefits
      from the same shape because re-fetching content per category
      keeps streaming output coherent."
    - When called with a `category` parameter, the install service
      writes all `would-create` files directly to disk itself and
      returns a small manifest — an array of steps with `filePath`,
      `status` (now `"created"`, `"overwritten"`, or `"exists"`),
      and `name`, but NO `content` field. The consumer never sees
      file content and never needs to write new files itself. This
      eliminates the 50-100KB response problem that caused MCP-tool
      agents to resort to programmatic parsing via Node scripts
      instead of conversational interaction.
    - The consumer only writes files itself for content that
      requires intelligent merging — specifically MCP config, where
      the consumer must read the existing config, merge
      prescriptions, and write. For MCP steps, the category call
      still returns the prescription data (not file content) so the
      consumer can perform the merge. Brain root scaffolding
      (creating directories) is handled by the install service
      directly.
    - IDE steps that require external tooling (e.g., VS Code
      extension install via CLI) return instructions for the
      consumer to execute, since those aren't simple file writes.
  undesired:
    - An install that prompts the user when there is nothing to
      overwrite. A category whose plan is entirely would-create
      steps must be applied silently — asking permission to write
      files that do not yet exist is the exact friction this
      service exists to remove, regardless of which consumer drives
      the run.
    - An install that silently overwrites existing files without
      asking. The fast path is for creates only; overwrites are
      destructive and must pause the consumer so the user can
      consent category-by-category.
    - "A category that bundles creates and overwrites and silently
      applies both because most of its files are new. If any file
      in the category would overwrite, the category pauses — the
      user is informed about the overwrite and decides whether to
      apply the whole category."
    - An install that asks conversational free-text questions
      instead of using the consumer's native structured-prompt
      mechanism for the overwrite and inherent-decision paths.
      Every pause point must use defined labels the user can
      select — `AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool path, structured
      stdin prompts in the CLI path — not open-ended text prompts.
    - An install that skips the inherent-decision prompts (brain
      root path, MCP config merge, IDE choice) because the
      fast-path rule was misapplied. Those prompts are not about
      file-writes and must always happen.
    - An install that silently assumes a brain root path from a
      sibling folder, environment variable, or platform default
      without asking the user. Discovery hints are useful for
      suggesting a default, but the consumer must confirm the path
      with the user.
    - An install service that writes MCP config entries directly,
      bypassing the consumer. The prescription pattern exists so
      the consumer has full visibility and the user's existing
      servers are never at risk.
    - An install that allows skipping the brain entirely. AIDE
      requires a brain — research and retros depend on it. The
      user chooses where, not whether.
    - "A consumer invoking only \"category: brain\" with a resolved
      brainPath and declaring the brain fully wired. That single
      invocation scaffolds the brain root directories and seeds
      content but leaves the brain MCP prescription unapplied —
      pipeline agents that declare mcpServers: [brain] cannot reach
      the brain, and the brain MCP server fails to launch when
      invoked because no entry exists in .mcp.json (boot reporter
      surfaces this as `no-mcp-entry` per the four-state vocabulary).
      The symmetric failure also applies: a consumer invoking only
      \"category: mcp\" with a brainPath patches .mcp.json but
      never scaffolds the brain root, leaving the brain MCP server
      pointed at an unprovisioned directory whose required sections
      (the coding-playbook section and the research section) cannot
      be probed for read rights — the brain is wired end-to-end but
      the navigation surface the agents need is missing. Both
      categories must run; treating either alone as sufficient
      produces a half-wired brain."
    - "Reintroducing the retired `invalid-path` brain status — under
      that name or any rename — by stat-ing the brain root path
      inside the install service or the boot reporter. Path validity
      is the launcher's job at MCP server startup, not a planner
      concern; the four boot-reporter states are `ok`, `no-brain-aide`,
      `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift`, and the install service must
      not teach a consumer to expect a fifth."
    - An install that clobbers the MCP config when it encounters
      malformed JSON. The consumer handles this case by asking the
      user, not by overwriting.
    - An install service that returns a pre-formatted prose summary
      instead of structured data. Both consumers need structured
      data to drive their flow — not a string they have to parse or
      parrot.
    - A category call that returns full file content for the
      consumer to write. The category call must write files to disk
      itself and return only a manifest. Returning content causes
      MCP-tool agents to receive 13-50KB responses that get
      persisted to disk as JSON files, which the agent then tries
      to parse with Node scripts instead of using the Write tool
      per file.
    - An install response that embeds full file content for all
      steps in a single payload. Even when steps are structured,
      a 50-100KB blob causes MCP-tool agents to write Python
      scripts to parse it instead of driving the install
      interactively. Content must be deferred to category-specific
      follow-up calls.
    - An install that appends the pointer stub on every run, fails
      the whole run on a single step failure, hardcodes brain root
      paths, or reaches for the network — regardless of which entry
      point invoked it. The idempotency, fail-soft, no-network, and
      no-hardcoded-path invariants live at the install-service
      layer so both consumers inherit them identically.
    - "A new install category added without a corresponding upgrade
      category. The upgrade surface must mirror the install surface
      — every category the install action installs must have an
      upgrade path. The two category lists (InitCategory and
      UpgradeCategory) are currently independent; adding to one
      without the other creates silent drift."
    - "A standalone CLI entry point that teaches AIDE doctrine
      instead of installing. The npx CLI is permitted because it
      is a thin invocation of channel two, exposing the same
      install service the `aide_init` MCP tool exposes; a CLI that
      walks the user through the methodology interactively would
      be the forbidden third channel from the trunk spec, not a
      thin invocation of channel two."
---

## Context

Every new project that adopts AIDE has to cross the same cold-start
gap: a short pointer to AIDE in the host config file the agent reads
on every session, the full methodology installed somewhere the agent
can crawl on demand, pipeline slash commands scaffolded, pipeline
agent definitions installed, pipeline skill templates installed, the
aide MCP server wired into the host's MCP config, the brain layer
provisioned so pipeline agents can persist and retrieve domain
knowledge, the brain MCP entry wired alongside the aide entry, a
README at the project root with the AIDE badge, and the IDE taught to
render `.aide` files as Markdown. Without a single install action that
does all of this, each project rediscovers the list and gets one or
two steps wrong — a stub written to the wrong file, a doc set
installed to a path the stub doesn't name, a slash command with a
typo, an MCP entry registered under a key the framework's agents
don't reference, no brain for the research agent to write to, or an
IDE that still renders `.aide` as plain text. This service exists to
collapse that cold-start gap into one invocation, regardless of which
entry point invoked it, so the decision to adopt AIDE in a new
project is trivially reversible and costs nothing to try. The
`aide_init` MCP tool is the entry point an agent calls when the user
is already talking to a model that connected to this server; the npx
CLI is the entry point a developer runs when they have nothing
connected yet. Both compile down to the same install service
contract.

The previous consumer-driven design corrected the biggest failure
mode of the original monolithic shape — silent overwrites of
malformed MCP configs, brains silently created at sibling defaults —
by pausing the consumer category-by-category and asking the user to
confirm each one before writing. That was the right instinct for
destructive writes. It was the wrong tax for creates. The observed
friction in cold-start runs is a user watching the consumer ask
"create `.aide/docs/`? create `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`?
create `.claude/agents/aide/aide-architect.md`?" for seven
consecutive categories of files that do not yet exist — nothing to
preserve, nothing to clobber, no decision the user can meaningfully
make beyond "yes, I ran the install, apply it." Every one of those
prompts is bureaucratic overhead on a path where the user has
already committed.

This spec refines the confirmation model by splitting the install
service's decisions into two kinds. **File-write decisions** are
resolvable from disk state: the service can stat every target path
and know with certainty whether a given category would create new
files, overwrite existing ones, or do nothing. When every step in a
category would create (nothing on disk, nothing to preserve), the
category has no decision the user can inform — the right answer is
to apply it silently. **Inherent decisions** are the ones disk state
cannot resolve: framework choice (two frameworks can coexist on disk
and the user alone knows which one the project actually targets),
brain root location (the user is the only one who knows where their
knowledge lives), whether to merge the AIDE servers into their
existing MCP config (the user's other servers are in that file), and
which IDEs they actually want configured (tooling they don't use is
noise). These always prompt, regardless of what is on disk, because
the question cannot be answered by reading the filesystem.

The shift is behavioral, not architectural. The install service
still plans every step, still returns structured data, still
executes writes itself via apply mode when called with a category —
nothing about the two-call pattern changes for the MCP-tool
consumer, and the CLI consumer drives the same plan/apply boundary
in-process instead of across a wire. What changes is the consumer's
branch logic over the plan: a category whose plan is entirely
would-create steps is applied silently and reported after the fact;
a category whose plan contains at least one would-overwrite step
pauses the consumer with the affected files named so the user can
make an informed choice about their own existing work; inherent
decisions always prompt because they are not file-write decisions at
all. A fully initialized project, re-run through either entry point,
produces a plan that is entirely `exists` steps, zero prompts, and a
terse "nothing to change" report. A fresh project produces a plan
that is entirely `would-create` file steps plus three-or-four
inherent prompts, and the user is interrupted only for those.

Methodology delivery inside this service is split across two peer
helpers on purpose. `writeMethodology` installs the short pointer
stub into the host's config file so the agent learns in its very
first read of the session that AIDE exists and where the full
material lives. `installMethodologyDocs` installs the full
material — the canonical methodology docs plus an index file —
into a dedicated doc-set folder on the host's disk, so the agent
reads only what the current task requires and only when the task
requires it. The split mirrors the methodology's own progressive-
disclosure rule at the delivery layer: the high-traffic surface
teaches where to look, the low-traffic surface holds the actual
content. Both land through the same silent-on-create path when
nothing of the user's is at risk.

The brain layer is the pipeline's shared memory. Pipeline agents
that declare `mcpServers: [brain]` reach this layer through a fixed
MCP key — `brain` — and the bytes that launch whatever knowledge
store sits behind that key are owned by the user, not the package.
The framework's brain contract is exactly two required entry-point
artifacts — a playbook entry-point artifact and a research
entry-point artifact — both reachable from the brain root the
user's MCP server points at; nothing else is required. Without a
brain root, both required entry-point artifacts, and a `brain` MCP
server entry, those agents silently lose brain access — their
research vanishes, retros are never persisted, and the pipeline
cannot learn across runs. The install action provisions this layer
in two pieces. First, it scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the
user-owned config file whose frontmatter (`name` +
`mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`) declares how the brain MCP
server launches and whose body carries three named marker-bounded
sections (`prose`, `playbook`, `research`) the install service
later reads through `parseBrainAide` to source the playbook and
research entry-point artifact bytes onto the brain root. Second, it
prescribes the brain MCP server entry — keyed `brain`, with
`command` and `args` derived from the scaffolded brain.aide via
`parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs` — for the consumer to merge
into the project's MCP config alongside the aide server. The
brain root path is resolved through the consumer's interview with
the user — never hardcoded, never silently defaulted — because the
brain root path is an inherent decision and the rule that covers
it is "always prompt," not "apply silently when nothing is on
disk." Once `.aide/config/` contains a scaffolded brain.aide, the
directory is user-owned forever: subsequent install runs never
return `would-overwrite` for any path under `.aide/config/`, and
changing the brain's launcher command, retargeting its inline path,
or editing the prose, playbook, or research body sections is a
user-side edit followed by `npx aidemd-mcp sync`, not a package
upgrade.

## Strategy

**The install service plans with a four-status enum; the consumer
branches on status distribution per category.** Each step in the
plan reports `would-create` (target path does not exist),
`would-overwrite` (target path exists and the canonical bytes
differ from what is on disk), `would-skip` (target path exists and
the canonical bytes match, so no write is needed), or `exists`
(step is already satisfied, nothing to do). The consumer walks the
plan category by category and picks one of three branches: if the
category's steps are all `would-create` (or a mix of
`would-create`, `would-skip`, and `exists`), apply silently — there
is no decision the user can inform, and every prompt on that path
is friction the spec exists to remove. If any step in the category
is `would-overwrite`, pause and ask, naming the files so the user
can decide whether to overwrite their own modified material. This
is how the spec's outcomes.desired rule "a pure-create category is
applied silently; a category with overwrites pauses" cashes out in
consumer-visible behavior. The agent in the MCP-tool consumer
drives this branching across the wire; the CLI consumer drives it
in-process — the rule is identical.

**`would-overwrite` is computed by byte comparison, not
heuristics.** Each helper stats its target path; if the file
exists, it reads the on-disk bytes and compares them to the
canonical content it was about to write. Identical bytes ⇒
`would-skip` (the file is already what we would have written, no
need to write again). Different bytes ⇒ `would-overwrite` (the
user has modified this file, or a prior version of the install
service wrote something that has since drifted). Missing file ⇒
`would-create`. The rule is deliberately strict: "same name but
different content" is enough to pause the consumer, because the
install service cannot tell a user-authored customization from a
stale previous install, and the safer branch is to ask. This
closes the outcomes.undesired line "a category that bundles
creates and overwrites and silently applies both because most of
its files are new" — even one overwrite in a category tips the
whole category into the prompt branch.

**Seed-semantic idempotency is an explicit exception, not a
default.** Three classes of files are user-owned the moment they
land and use a presence-only check instead of byte comparison:
`.aide/config/brain.aide` (and any future file under
`.aide/config/`), the playbook entry-point artifact, and the
research entry-point artifact. For these, the helper checks
whether the file exists at the expected path and reports `exists`
when it does — no byte comparison against the bundled default or
against the brain.aide section that originated the bytes, because
both surfaces are the user's to evolve the moment the brain comes
alive. For everything else (methodology docs, slash commands,
agent definitions, skill templates, the AIDE pointer stub region,
the aide MCP entry), byte comparison is the correct idempotency
signal because those files are the package's canonical bytes
delivered into the host. The seed-vs-canonical split is the
forcing function that keeps user customization safe in the brain
layer while keeping the package authoritative everywhere it
should be.

**Inherent-decision prompts are orthogonal to the file-write
branch.** Framework confirmation, brain root location,
MCP-config merge into an existing config, and IDE choice always
pause the consumer regardless of the plan's status distribution.
The rule "silent on create, prompt on overwrite" governs
file-writes only; inherent decisions are not file-writes, they are
choices about what the user wants their environment to look like,
and the filesystem cannot resolve them. A fully-initialized
project re-run still asks zero questions because the categories
land as `exists` *and* the inherent decisions have already been
answered on disk — brain root path baked into the registered MCP
server entry, IDE config present, framework already detected — so
the consumer has no new questions to ask. A fresh project asks
three or four (framework confirm, brain root path, MCP merge
approval, IDE choice) and nothing else. This closes the
outcomes.undesired line "an install that skips the
inherent-decision prompts because the fast-path rule was
misapplied" — the rule does not apply to them in the first place.

**Apply mode writes creates immediately and defers overwrites to
user consent.** When the consumer invokes the install service
with a category parameter, the apply helper walks the category's
steps: every `would-create` step is written to disk straight
through and returned with status `"created"`; `would-skip` and
`exists` steps pass through unchanged; `would-overwrite` steps
are only written when the consumer explicitly signaled user
approval for the category (the consumer does this by proceeding
to the apply call — if the user had declined, the consumer would
never have made it). On approval, the overwrite happens and the
step is returned with status `"overwritten"`. The manifest that
comes back carries `"created"`, `"overwritten"`, and `"exists"`
distinctly, so the consumer can report a truthful breakdown —
"created 8, overwrote 1, kept 3 as-is" — not a vague "done."
This satisfies the outcomes.desired line about manifest statuses
and the outcomes.undesired line about silent clobbers (writes
happen, but only when the plan already showed them as overwrites
and the consumer already got approval).

**Category isolation: one category's decision never mutates
another.** Each helper (methodology, commands, agents, skills,
mcp, brain, ide, readme, framework) produces its plan from its
own canonical content and its own disk inspection. No shared
state, no cross-category coupling, no ordering dependency beyond
the consumer's presentation order. This matches the orchestrator/
helper pattern the codebase already uses (orchestrator
coordinates, helpers do one thing) and is the forcing function
that makes per-category pauses work — the consumer can apply
methodology silently, pause on commands because the user has a
modified slash command, apply agents silently, and the categories
do not contaminate each other. A category's failure surfaces as a
single category's failure in the report; it does not abort the
rest. This closes the outcomes.desired "every step is idempotent"
line at the category-boundary level too.

**MCP merge is an inherent-decision wrapper around file-write
prescription.** The install service never writes MCP config. It
returns the server key name and the entry object the config
needs; the consumer reads the existing config, shows the user the
planned merge, and writes. The "show the user before writing"
pause is an inherent-decision prompt — the user's other servers
live in that file, the install service cannot know which ones the
user wants preserved, and the filesystem state of `.mcp.json`
does not answer the question. Malformed JSON hits the same path:
the consumer surfaces the parse error and asks how to proceed
rather than silently replacing the file. This closes the
outcomes.undesired lines about clobbering MCP configs and writing
MCP entries directly, and honors the parent spec's `src/.aide`
invariant that the install service never touches MCP config.
Sync (`npx aidemd-mcp sync`) is the only mutation surface for
`.mcp.json` in the entire package, and the install service does
not bypass it on the install path either.

**The brain MCP key is fixed at `brain` and its command/args are
derived from the user-owned `brain.aide` via `parseBrainAide`.**
The MCP entry the install service prescribes is always keyed
`brain`, regardless of what launcher the entry carries
underneath. The launcher bytes themselves — `command` and
`args` — are not owned by this package: `provisionBrain` reads
`.aide/config/brain.aide` (the in-memory template bytes for a
cold install, the on-disk content for a re-run) through
`parseBrainAide`, runs `interpolateArgs` over the parsed
`mcpServerConfig.args`, and emits the resulting object as the
prescription. The package never constructs a launcher inline,
never introspects args to infer backend identity, never branches
on the brain's `name` label, and never owns a backend registry,
matcher table, or driver enum — those code-level dispatch
surfaces were retired and the parser actively rejects any
frontmatter field beyond `name` and `mcpServerConfig` that would
bring them back. Adding, retargeting, or replacing a brain is a
user-side edit to `.aide/config/brain.aide` followed by
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` — never a code-level extension to this
package. Legacy installs that wrote the entry under the old
`obsidian` key are migrated to `brain` exclusively through
`provisionBrain`'s MCP step, which emits a `would-overwrite`
step on the next `/aide:upgrade` or `/aide:init` run. The CLI
never silently rewrites a user's `obsidian` key, and no runtime
fallback to the legacy key exists anywhere else in the codebase.
See `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` for the
derivation pipeline and migration branches, and
`src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` for the single canonical
reader contract.

**The brain layer is required; root location is inherent;
scaffolding is silent-on-create; `.aide/config/` is user-owned
directory-wide.** AIDE is opinionated that the pipeline needs
persistent memory. The consumer asks the user where their brain
root is, offering discovered hints (env var, sibling
`my-brain/`, prior install) as suggestions the user can pick or
override — the user cannot skip the brain layer, only choose
where it lives. Once the path is resolved, the brain category's
scaffolding plan covers exactly the surfaces the framework
contract names: `.aide/config/brain.aide` (the launcher config
and the source of the entry-point artifact bytes), the brain
root directories, the playbook entry-point artifact, and the
research entry-point artifact — the two required entry-point
artifacts ship together because both must be present for agents
to satisfy the brain contract. Each step follows its idempotency
rule (presence-only for the seed-semantic three above, byte-
compare for the derived MCP entry) and reports accordingly. The
entry-point artifact bytes themselves come from the `playbook`
and `research` body sections of the scaffolded brain.aide via
`parseBrainAide` — the package does not own those bytes as
inline TypeScript constants, and the legacy `*_TEMPLATE`
top-level constants in provisionBrain's `index.ts` were retired
to enforce that. After the initial scaffold, every path under
`.aide/config/` is user-owned forever: the install pipeline
never returns `would-overwrite` for any file in that directory
on subsequent runs, and the ownership boundary is structural
(directory-level), not a per-file allowlist. Editing brain.aide
to swap the launcher, retarget the inline path, or rewrite the
prose, playbook, or research body sections is a user-side edit;
the package never re-touches those bytes. The inherent prompt
(where is the brain root?) and the silent-on-create write (seed
the config and the two entry-point artifacts once the path is
known) are cleanly separated. This closes the
outcomes.undesired lines about skipping the brain, about
silently adopting a sibling default, and about the install
pipeline mutating user-owned config bytes after first scaffold.

**The framework's brain contract is exactly two required
entry-point artifacts.** Playbook and research — nothing else.
Anything more (a brain-root CLAUDE.md, a journal directory, a
kanban board, an identity file) is the user's choice and the
user's edit to brain.aide; provisionBrain plans no InitStep for
any of them. A previous shape scaffolded a brain-root CLAUDE.md
as part of the brain contract; that step is retired and any
reintroduction under any rename ("brain-root CLAUDE.md",
"navigation entry-point") is rejected, because the methodology
guidance that lived in that file (link-crawling protocols,
decision protocols, where-to-find-things tables) now ships
through pipeline prompts where the strategist owns the prose,
not through install code. Keeping the contract minimal is what
lets the install service stay storage-agnostic — every brain
this package wires into needs the two entry-point artifacts to
be reachable for agents to satisfy the navigation pattern, and
no other artifact is structural enough to justify shipping by
default.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across the install service's
identifiers and prose.** The brain layer is described in
structural terms — "brain root", "playbook entry-point
artifact", "research entry-point artifact" — never in
connector-specific terms. How those structural concepts manifest
in the user's chosen storage (Markdown files in an editor-backed
brain, pages in a hosted knowledge base, plain folders) is
brain.aide's concern, not the package's. The bundled default
brain.aide ships an editor-backed Markdown body because that is
the most common storage choice, but that is a template-content
fact (a user-owned byte the moment it lands), not a code-level
special case the install service branches on. The retired
storage-specific identifiers (the `Vault*` family, `*_DIRS`
constants named for one storage shape, hub-suffixed type
fields, references to "wikilinks" or "hub notes" in JSDoc and
test fixtures) were renamed across the install service code in
the same cycle that collapsed the schema. The two allowed
exceptions live elsewhere in the tree and do not bleed into
this orchestrator's surface: the bundled-template helper folder
name (a content anchor, not a dispatch axis) and the
`.obsidian` directory heuristic comment in `brainRootExists`
(an honest implementation detail in a sibling helper).
Anti-example references inside undesired-outcome blocks of
specs in the install subtree are the third allowed exception
and follow the same carve-out as the root spec.

**Framework detection is a read-only inspection whose result is
always confirmed.** Framework choice is inherent — two
frameworks can coexist on disk, and the user alone knows which
one matters for this project. The detector returns its best
guess and the consumer confirms with the user; the user can
accept or override. Detection never writes. The confirmation
prompt happens even when the detection is unambiguous because
the user is the source of truth about their own project, not
the presence of a `.claude/` directory.

**IDE configuration is per-IDE and always asked.** The user's
IDE preferences are inherent — a developer on VS Code has no
use for a Zed settings file, and vice versa. The consumer asks
which IDEs to configure; it only applies what the user
confirms. IDE steps that need external tooling (VS Code
extension install via CLI) return an `instructions` field the
consumer can execute rather than attempting the shell call from
inside the install service, because the install service has no
guarantee the CLI is on PATH and "the rest of the install
succeeds when IDE fails" requires that IDE failure be
reportable, not crashing.

**Network is never the happy path.** Every asset the install
action needs is checked into the repo — the methodology docs,
the command templates, the agent definitions, the skill
templates, the bundled VS Code `.vsix`, the bundled default
brain.aide template. A developer running the install on a
laptop with no network gets the same result as a developer
running it online, and the silent-on-create branch is safe
because the canonical content is local and deterministic.

**The upgrade surface must mirror the install surface, category
for category.** Every new install category has a corresponding
upgrade category, and the two lists are maintained together.
The outcomes.undesired line naming drift between
`InitCategory` and `UpgradeCategory` is a deliberate guardrail —
a category the install action installs but upgrade does not
track is silently frozen at install time, and a category
upgrade tracks without an install counterpart is unreachable on
cold start. Keep them in lockstep.

**Two consumers, one wire shape per consumer.** The MCP-tool
consumer (`src/tools/init/`) translates the install service's
plan into MCP responses and uses `AskUserQuestion` for
inherent-decision and overwrite prompts. The CLI consumer
(`src/cli/init/`) translates the same plan into terminal output
and uses stdin prompts for the same decisions. Neither consumer
is allowed to inline install logic that the other consumer
cannot reach — every category invariant (idempotency,
prescription pattern, planner/apply split, status enum) lives
in the install service, and the consumers are thin adapters
over it. A bug fix in the install service fixes both consumers;
a fix that has to happen in both consumers is a sign the fix
belonged in the service.

## Good examples

**Cold start on a fresh project via the MCP-tool consumer — four
prompts, nothing else.** The agent calls `aide_init` with no
category. The install service returns a plan with every step in
`methodology`, `commands`, `agents`, `skills`, the `.aide/` tree,
and `readme` as `would-create`; the aide MCP step returns its
prescription and the brain steps return placeholders because no
brain root is resolved yet; the IDE category returns its
`would-create` steps.

The agent opens with:

> I detected Claude Code. Is that right?

The user confirms via `AskUserQuestion`. The agent, seeing every
file category is pure `would-create`, applies methodology,
commands, agents, skills, the `.aide/` tree, and the README scaffold
one after another without another prompt, reporting briefly after
each:

> Installed methodology — canonical docs under `.aide/docs/` and a pointer stub in `CLAUDE.md`.
> Installed pipeline slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`.
> Installed pipeline agent definitions under `.claude/agents/aide/`.
> Installed study-playbook skill under `.claude/skills/`.
> Scaffolded `.aide/intent.aide` for the project root.
> Created `README.md` with the AIDE badge at the top.

Then the inherent prompts:

> AIDE needs a brain layer. I found a candidate at `D:/Code/Me/my-brain/` (sibling folder) and one at `$BRAIN` (env var -> `D:/Notes/main-brain/`). Pick one, or type a path.

The user picks the env-var hint. The agent invokes the install
service with `category: "brain"` and that path; the install
service scaffolds `.aide/config/brain.aide` (the user-owned
launcher config + the three marker-bounded body sections that
seed the entry-point artifacts), the brain root directories, the
playbook entry-point artifact (sourced from the `playbook` body
section of the freshly scaffolded brain.aide via `parseBrainAide`),
and the research entry-point artifact (sourced from the `research`
body section the same way), returning `"created"` for each. Then:

> Your `.mcp.json` has `github` and `memory` configured. I'll add `aide` and `brain` alongside them. OK?

The user confirms; the agent merges and writes. Then:

> I can configure Zed and VS Code for `.aide` preview. Want both, one, or neither?

The user picks Zed only; the agent applies the Zed step silently,
skips VS Code. Total prompts from a cold start: four (framework,
brain root, MCP merge, IDE choice). Zero prompts about
file-creates.

**Cold start via the npx CLI consumer — same four prompts,
terminal shape.** A developer with no AIDE-aware agent connected
runs `npx aidemd-mcp@latest init` in their project directory. The
CLI invokes the install service in-process and gets back the same
plan the MCP tool would have received. The CLI prints:

> Detected framework: Claude Code. [Y/n]

The developer presses Enter. The CLI walks the file categories
silently (all `would-create`), prints a per-category report after
each apply call, then asks the same brain-root, MCP-merge, and IDE
prompts via stdin instead of `AskUserQuestion`. Same plan, same
status enum, same idempotency, same outcome on disk — only the
wire shape differs. A developer who runs `aide_init` later from
inside a connected agent's session sees every category as `exists`
because the install service already finished the work.

**Re-run on a fully initialized project — zero prompts, either
consumer.** A week later the developer re-invokes the install
action — could be the agent calling `aide_init`, could be `npx
aidemd-mcp@latest init` from the terminal. Every file category
comes back entirely as `exists`. The aide and brain MCP entries
are present in `.mcp.json`. The brain scaffold is intact (the
brain.aide file under `.aide/config/` is detected by presence,
the entry-point artifacts at the brain root the same way). The
consumer reports once and stops:

> This project is already fully initialized — methodology, commands, agents, skills, `.aide` tree, README, MCP servers, brain layer, and Zed preview all present. Nothing to change.

No prompt fires (no `AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool path, no
stdin prompt in the CLI path) because the plan showed nothing to
decide.

**User edited brain.aide; subsequent install picks up the edit
without prompting.** The developer hand-edits
`.aide/config/brain.aide` to retarget the brain root by changing
the inline path string in `mcpServerConfig.args`. They run
`npx aidemd-mcp sync` to push the new launcher into `.mcp.json`,
then re-run the install action. The brain config step shows
`exists` (presence-only check on the user-owned file under
`.aide/config/`); the brain root directory step reports against
the new path; the playbook and research entry-point artifacts at
the new root either show `exists` (if the user already moved
them) or `would-create` with content sourced from the edited
brain.aide's `playbook` and `research` body sections — never from
an inline package default. The MCP step's expected entry is
derived from the edited brain.aide via `parseBrainAide` +
`interpolateArgs` and matches what sync just wrote, so it shows
`exists` too. The user's customization flows through every
downstream step automatically; the package never proposes
overwriting it.

**Partial overwrite — one file named, one prompt, one category.**
The developer has edited `.claude/commands/aide/research.md` to
add a line of their own instructions. A release later they
re-invoke the install action. The plan comes back with the
`commands` category mixed: every other slash command lands as
`would-skip` (canonical bytes match), and `research.md` lands as
`would-overwrite`. Every other category is pure `would-create` or
pure `exists`.

The consumer applies methodology silently (all `would-create`),
applies agents silently, applies skills silently. At commands it
pauses:

> The `commands` category has one file that would overwrite your existing version: `.claude/commands/aide/research.md`. The other slash commands are either already current or missing and would be freshly installed. Overwrite your modified `research.md`?

Options: "Overwrite it" / "Keep my version and install the
others" / "Skip the whole category." The MCP-tool consumer
surfaces these via `AskUserQuestion`; the CLI consumer surfaces
them via a structured stdin prompt with the same three labels.
The user picks the middle option. The consumer invokes the
install service with `category: "commands"` and signals that the
overwrite is declined; the install service writes only the
`would-create` steps, returns the `would-overwrite` step with
status unchanged, and the consumer reports:

> Installed the other slash commands under `.claude/commands/aide/`. Kept your modified `research.md` as-is.

One prompt, surgically scoped to the one file that actually
needed a decision — same outcome regardless of which consumer
drove the run.

**Legacy install with the `obsidian` MCP key — one prompt,
migration is visible.** The project was originally initialized
under an older release that wrote the brain MCP entry under the
key `obsidian` instead of `brain`. A developer re-runs the
install action. Every file category lands as `exists`; the brain
file-write steps are seed-semantic and presence-only. The MCP
step computes the expected entry from the user's brain.aide
(unchanged since the original install) and notices that the
canonical key `brain` is absent while the legacy `obsidian` key
holds the equivalent entry. The MCP step reports
`would-overwrite` with prescription `{ key: "brain", entry:
<derived> }`. The consumer pauses:

> Your `.mcp.json` carries the brain entry under the legacy `obsidian` key. The new key is `brain`. Move the entry to `brain`?

The user confirms. The consumer merges, writes `.mcp.json` with
the entry under `brain`, drops the `obsidian` key, and reports
the migration. The package never silently rewrites the user's
config — every key change passes through the consumer's pause.

## Bad examples

**Prompting on pure-create categories.** Cold start, fresh
project, and the consumer asks:

> Methodology — this will create the CLAUDE.md pointer stub and the canonical methodology docs in `.aide/docs/`. Want me to set this up?

Then:

> Commands — this will create the pipeline slash commands in `.claude/commands/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

Then:

> Agents — this will create the pipeline agent definitions in `.claude/agents/aide/`. Want me to set these up?

The user hits "yes" repeatedly in a row for files that do not yet
exist, staring at a wizard that is asking permission to do the
thing they invoked it to do. This is the exact friction the
confirmation model removes; the category-by-category pause belongs
to overwrites, not creates. Same failure mode whether the consumer
asks via `AskUserQuestion` or via a stdin prompt — the install
service's plan told the consumer there was nothing to decide, and
the consumer asked anyway.

**Silent overwrite because "most of the category is new."** A
developer has a customized `research.md` from the previous
example. The consumer heuristically decides the `commands`
category is "mostly creates (the rest are new, one overwrite)"
and applies the whole category without pausing, overwriting the
user's modifications. The consumer reports "installed the slash
commands" and the user discovers tomorrow that their custom
instructions are gone. The rule is strict: even one
`would-overwrite` step in a category tips the category into the
prompt branch.

**Skipping an inherent-decision prompt because the "fast path"
applies.** The consumer sees a `my-brain/` directory sibling to
the project, decides that counts as the brain layer being
"already present," skips the brain-root question, and wires the
project's MCP config to that sibling. The user's actual AIDE
brain root is at `D:/Notes/aide-brain/` and the sibling is an old
experiment. The inherent-decision rule exists precisely for this
case: the brain root path is not resolvable from disk state, so
the consumer must ask regardless of what is on disk.

**Silent MCP merge.** The consumer finds `.mcp.json` on disk with
two existing servers, assumes the merge is a file-write decision,
and writes the new config without pausing. In the best case the
user is surprised; in the worst case the existing config was
malformed and the consumer has just replaced it with a "clean"
version that silently dropped the user's custom servers. MCP
merge is inherent — always prompt, in every consumer.

**One summary table, one blanket confirmation, zero
granularity.** The consumer returns:

> Here's the full plan: methodology (creates pending), commands (creates pending), agents (creates pending), skills (create pending), MCP (1 prescription), brain (pending root path), IDE (creates pending). Apply all?

The user says yes and loses their modified `research.md` to a
silent overwrite they had no way to see in the table. The
per-category pause model exists so overwrites surface against
their own category, with the affected files named — not buried in
a global "apply all."

**Prompts fired via free-text instead of structured options.**
The consumer writes:

> Do you want me to go ahead with methodology? (yes/no)

The user types "y" and the consumer parses it. This violates the
structured-options rule from outcomes.undesired — every pause
point must use defined labels (`AskUserQuestion` in the MCP-tool
consumer, structured stdin prompts in the CLI consumer), because
free-text prompts cost the agent a parse turn and the CLI a
fragile string match. Both consumers have native structured-
prompt mechanisms; the install service's invariant is that the
consumer uses them.

**Reporting that does not distinguish created from
overwritten.** After an apply call that wrote several new files
and overwrote one, the consumer reports:

> Done — installed all files.

The user has no way to know which file was theirs and got
replaced. The manifest statuses exist so the consumer can say
"created N, overwrote 1: research.md" — collapsing them into a
single undifferentiated count defeats the purpose of the status
enum.

**Byte-comparing brain.aide and prompting "overwrite your brain
config?".** The user edited `.aide/config/brain.aide` to retarget
the brain root. On the next install run, the brain config step
reads brain.aide off disk, byte-compares it against the bundled
default template, sees a divergence, and reports
`would-overwrite`. The consumer pauses with "overwrite your brain
config?". A reflexive Enter replaces the user's customized
brain.aide with the stale package default, losing the
retargeting. Files under `.aide/config/` are user-owned
directory-wide and use presence-only idempotency; they may never
report `would-overwrite`. The same failure class applies to the
playbook and research entry-point artifacts at the brain root —
both are seed-semantic, both presence-only.

**Inlining entry-point artifact bytes back into the install
service code.** A future maintainer "simplifies" provisionBrain
by reintroducing a top-level `PLAYBOOK_TEMPLATE` constant in
`index.ts` and pushing those bytes into the playbook InitStep,
bypassing the read of brain.aide's `playbook` body section
through `parseBrainAide`. A user who edited their brain.aide's
`playbook` section to add their own routing rows finds the next
install ignores their edit and lands the package default again.
The schema-collapse cycle retired exactly this regression for
launcher bytes; reintroducing it for entry-point artifact bytes
is the same regression in different clothing. brain.aide is the
single source of truth for both launcher bytes and entry-point
artifact bytes; nothing in this service may compete with it.

**Reintroducing a brain-root CLAUDE.md scaffolding step.** A
maintainer reads an old release note, sees that the brain-root
CLAUDE.md "used to ship by default", and adds a step back into
provisionBrain that scaffolds a navigation file at the brain
root under any name — `CLAUDE.md`, "brain-root navigation",
"navigation entry-point". The framework's brain contract is
exactly two required entry-point artifacts (playbook + research)
and nothing else; reintroducing a third required artifact under
any rename restores the retired contract and forces every user
to either accept the package's navigation prose or hand-delete
it on every brain. Methodology guidance about how agents
navigate the brain lives in pipeline prompts where the
strategist owns the prose, not in install code.

**Constructing the brain MCP entry inline instead of deriving it
from brain.aide.** A maintainer "speeds up" the MCP step by
hardcoding `command: "npx"` and `args: ["@bitbonsai/mcpvault",
brainPath]` directly inside provisionBrain and skipping the
`parseBrainAide` + `interpolateArgs` pass. The user's
brain.aide — which may carry a different launcher, a different
inline path, or a custom platform branch — is ignored. The
package's compiled constant wins every time, defeating the
user-ownership model the schema collapse was built around. Every
byte the MCP step prescribes must trace back through
`parseBrainAide` to the user's brain.aide, not to a launcher
constant in this package.

**Reintroducing storage-specific identifiers in the install
service code.** A new helper named `vaultExists`, a constant
named `VAULT_DIRS`, a type field named `playbookHubPath`, JSDoc
that says "follow the wikilinks in the hub note", or a test
fixture that uses "vault" and "wikilink" as sentinels. The
mental model these names express is one storage shape's, not the
framework's structural shape. The forbidden-token sweep is
project-wide; the install subtree's identifiers and prose must
read in storage-agnostic terms ("brain root", "playbook
entry-point artifact", "research entry-point artifact"). The
allowed exceptions (the bundled-template helper folder name and
the `.obsidian` directory heuristic in `brainRootExists`) are
explicit and live in sibling helpers; bringing the retired
vocabulary back into this orchestrator's surface is a
regression.

**Surfacing a fifth brain state at the install layer.** A
helper "improves" reporting by `stat`-ing the brain root path
and returning a synthetic `invalid-path` status when the path
does not resolve. The install service must not teach a consumer
to expect a fifth boot-reporter state. The four states are `ok`,
`no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift`; path validity
is the launcher's job at MCP server startup, not a planner
concern. Reintroducing a fifth state under any name (`invalid-
path`, `unreachable-root`, `bad-target`) drags the planner back
into responsibilities it deliberately offloaded.

**A consumer that inlines install logic instead of going through
the service.** A maintainer adds a "post-install hook" feature
to the MCP-tool consumer that touches files outside the install
service's plan — say, optimistically running `npm install` after
the install action finishes. The CLI consumer doesn't get the
hook, the two consumers diverge, and a future developer running
the CLI sees different results than a developer using the agent.
The rule is that every category invariant lives in the install
service; if a behavior should affect both consumers, it belongs
in the service, not in either consumer.

**A standalone CLI verb that walks the user through methodology
instead of installing.** A maintainer adds `npx aidemd-mcp
teach` that interactively explains AIDE concepts to a developer
in the terminal, branching from the install path. The npx CLI
exists as a thin invocation of channel two — the install
action — and nothing more. A teaching CLI is the forbidden third
channel from the trunk spec, regardless of how thinly the verb
wraps the install service.

## References

- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/.aide/intent.aide` -- Canonical parent.
  Source of the two delivery channels (handshake + install
  action), the collapsed `brain.aide` schema (frontmatter =
  `name` + `mcpServerConfig`; body = three marker-bounded
  sections in fixed order), the marker-pair body grammar
  (`<!-- aide-prose-start -->`, `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->`,
  `<!-- aide-research-start -->` and their closers, lowercase
  case-sensitive), the retired in-code backend registry, the
  `.aide/config/` user-owned directory boundary, the four-state
  boot vocabulary (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`,
  `mcp-drift`), `npx aidemd-mcp sync` as the only mutation
  surface for `.mcp.json`, and the storage-agnostic vocabulary
  rule with the forbidden-token list. Anchors every Strategy
  paragraph that touches the brain layer, the MCP key
  derivation, the `.aide/config/` ownership rule, and the
  storage-agnostic identifier sweep.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/.aide` -- Immediate parent. Source
  of the client-parity invariant, the idempotency-as-invariant
  rule (lives at the install-service layer so both consumers
  inherit it), the "tool never touches MCP config" rule, and the
  two-entry-points-one-shared-service shape with the rejection
  of a teaching CLI as a forbidden third channel. Anchors the
  category-isolation paragraph, the MCP prescription paragraph,
  and the closing two-consumers paragraph.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` --
  Single canonical reader for brain.aide. Source of the contract
  that the install service derives both the brain MCP entry's
  `command`/`args` AND the playbook/research entry-point
  artifact bytes through `parseBrainAide` (frontmatter +
  marker-bounded body sections) + `interpolateArgs`, never from
  inline TypeScript constants. Anchors the "brain MCP key fixed
  at `brain`" and "entry-point artifact bytes from brain.aide"
  Strategy paragraphs and the inline-constants Bad example.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide`
  -- Sibling planner that owns the five brain-category steps
  (brain.aide, brain root directories, playbook entry-point
  artifact, research entry-point artifact, brain MCP entry).
  Source of the two-required-entry-point-artifacts contract, the
  `.aide/config/` directory-wide ownership inheritance, the
  seed-semantic-vs-canonical-derived idempotency split, the
  legacy `obsidian`-key migration branches, the retirement of
  the brain-root CLAUDE.md scaffolding step, and the
  storage-agnostic identifier convention used in code. Anchors
  the brain-layer Strategy paragraphs, the seed-semantic
  idempotency exception, the migration Good example, and the
  "reintroducing CLAUDE.md" / "fifth brain state" Bad
  examples.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/initContent/.aide`
  -- Sibling helper. Sole reader of `docs/` for the install
  subtree. Confirms the orchestrator/helper convention and the
  single-source-of-truth invariant the category-isolation
  paragraph rests on; also confirms that methodology-doc
  enumeration lives in the chokepoint registry rather than in
  downstream callers.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/installMethodologyDocs/.aide`
  -- Sibling planner that lands the canonical methodology doc set
  on the host's disk under `.aide/docs/`. Source of the per-file
  idempotency pattern and the byte-faithful-from-canonical
  delivery shape that the methodology-split paragraph in
  Context invokes.
- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide`
  -- Sibling planner that installs the short pointer stub into
  the host config file. Source of the marker-based stub
